APPLE IIGS SYTLEWRITER PRINTER DRIVER v1.0 	--		TECHNICAL DESCRIPTION (ERS)Written by Ben Koning (ben@apple.com x45806 408/738-1763)November 1991INTRODUCTIONThis is a printer driver for the Apple "StyleWriter" ink jet printer.  It is afeature-reduced printer driver, written in a very short time in order to get adriver out to the public as soon as possible.  It is based on a similarlyfeature-reduced HP DeskJet PLUS driver.  The philosophy is to be short on bellsand whistles and to be long on zero-embarrassment usability.DEVIATIONS FROM OTHER DUMB-DEVICE PRINTER DRIVERSCommunications is handled internally inside the driver, due to the specialway in which the 57.6K baud software-handshaking serial interface operates.The SCC channel A/B selection is inferred from the current printer-port driversettings: the PMGetPortName call is used; and a case-insensitive check is made forthe first few characters of the "Printer" or "Modem" filenames returned by thePMGetPortName call.Pages are recorded as pictures, rendered, and printed, one at a time; they are notsaved en masse for the entire document.  All copies of the current page are printedat PrClosePage time, like the LaserWriter driver.Picture data for the pages is spooled to disk if necessary, like the version 4.0or later ImageWriter and ImageWriter LQ drivers.  If the user booted over thenetwork, then the spool file is located in the correct {username} directory;otherwise, it is located in */System/Drivers.  The file name is TMP.xxxx, where"xxxx" is a 4-digit random hexadecimal number.  The file is deleted when finished.This driver contains all of the features of previous dumb-printer drivers, withthe following important exceptions:	* No color, and arbitrary grayscale support.  Standard black and standard	  white must exist in standard color palette locations; otherwise, color	  to black/white mapping will be incorrect.			* No landscape mode.		* No draft ("text only") mode.		* No reduction ("66% reduction", "33% reduction", etc) modes.		* The PrPixelMap call does not support printing of a subset of a pixelmap.	  Only the LocInfo structure is considered; the source rect is ignored.		* There is no notion of separate Page Setup and Print Job dialogs; they	  are the same but don't look the same.  Saves much code/complexity.All paper types mentioned in the Macintosh StyleWriter Driver ERS (by Suki Lee)are supported.Error handling, as described in the Macintosh StyleWriter ERS is supported bymeans of alert dialogs for all of the same conditions.SCALING STRATEGYThe driver scales the recorded page definition picture to 4.5x (360 dpi) or to2.25x (180 dpi).  Bitmaps are thus scaled; very rough patterns may exhibit someartifacts but usually look ok.  Graphic objects other than text are renderedcorrectly, since they are mathematically defined.  Text strings are scaled to 5x,however, to avoid Font Manager burden and to avoid total loss of visual fidelity.In order cause the 5x text strings to fit in the 4.5x space, and to deal withhorizontal font scaling error, line layout is performed.HORIZONTAL LINE LAYOUT FOR TEXTHorizontal line layout is implemented.  The intercharacter spacing of text stringsis manipulated so that a given string played back during the page picture renderingprocess is drawn with 90% of its original width.  This is done by calculating smalltemporary negative values for SpaceExtra and CharExtra for each individual string.Line layout thus solves the problems of slight horizontal font scaling error (causingoverlap, especially in text with face modifications (ie, bold, italic, etc)) andthe difference in device resolutions.Setting wDev bit 14 disables line layout. This bit is always cleared by defaultand can only be set by an application.VERTICAL ANTI-CLIPPING FOR TEXTThe notBIC transfer mode is used to draw text strings if the text transfer mode isset to modeCopy (default).  This makes text transfer behave in an OR-like fashionso that descenders are not clipped due to the scaled font being taller than theexact multiple of the original (application-requested) font height.  This willcause a problem for applications that rely on using the bounding box around textto erase its surroundings.  The notBIC transfer mode is substituted if theapplication's requested transfer mode is the default modeCopy; if it is anythingelse it is left alone.Setting wDev bit 13 disables the OR-ing text drawing mode behavior described above.This bit is always cleared by default and can only be set by an application.640 MODE PIXELMAP ANTI-BLURRINGFor pixelmaps in 640 mode, CopyPixels would normally cause loss of black and whitedetail while scaling up in order to keep dithered 2-bit color pixel pairs intact.Instead, StdPixels is patched out and the pixelmap is prescaled by the driver,which then calls StdPixels to do the actual transfer at 1:1 scaling.  This onlydone if 640 mode and only if high quality is selected.Setting wDev bit 12 disables special 640-mode pixelmap handling.  This bit isalways cleard by default and can only be set by an application.PAGE-AS-JOB HANDLINGIf a PrOpenDoc call happens immediately (within 5 seconds, based on TickCount) aftera PrCloseDoc call, then certain initialization tasks, such as the sending ofimmediate printer reset and the resetting of the page counter, are not performed.This is for those programs that choose to treat each page as an entirely separatedocument.This is necessary because the StyleWriter's reset command sequence interrupts theprinter from whatever it was previously doing, and so multiple-page documents couldnot be printed reliably otherwise.  One of the other benefits of this technique arethat the page counter in the status box works correctly with page-as-job applications.Setting wDev bit 15 disables the status message box displayed on top of thescreen; this bit is always cleared by default and can only be set by anapplication.SPEEDThe following measures are taken to enhance speed performance, in order ofimplementation:	* Trivial source pixel large-area whitespace elimination razor.	* Lookup table driven source to print pixel conversion with auto grayscale.	* QuickDraw text clip/draw code not executed if outside of band buffer.	* Multibank (>64K) band rendering buffer, if memory allows.	* Razor within compressor for repeating source pixel bytes.	* More efficient overall compressor code.	* Repeat-scanline opcode used for non-vertical-condense case.	* Draw-blank-scanline opcode used when possible.	* InstallWithStats font manager code not executed if outside of band buffer.	* Full black-case/white-case opcodes instead of using the repeat opcode.